25:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-why-history-helps",{"id":"01-why-history-helps","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Why the history is worth your time"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Every design decision in an operating system was an answer to the constraints of its time. When a computer cost more than a building, the overriding goal was to keep its one CPU busy every possible second, because idle hardware was money burning. When chips became cheap enough for every desk, the goal shifted to being friendly and responsive for a single human. On a phone, battery life and touch responsiveness dominate; in the cloud, packing many tenants safely onto one server is what pays the bills."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is why history is useful rather than trivia: the features you use every day are fossil records of old problems. Understanding the problem an idea solved makes the idea itself far easier to remember and to reason about when it breaks."}],["$","ul","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Expensive hardware era: never let the costly CPU idle, share it across jobs."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Cheap-hardware era: make one machine pleasant and safe for one person."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Mobile era: stretch a battery and respond instantly to a human finger."}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Cloud era: run many untrusting tenants on shared iron, isolated and metered."}]]}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Why it matters",": "]}],"Whenever an OS feature seems arbitrary, ask what was expensive when it was invented. The answer is almost always the real explanation."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L27",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch02%2F01-why-history-helps.jpg?v=97071ec103c27088eb6b202732b0ead2","alt":"Hand-drawn timeline arrow from \"expensive hardware\" through \"cheap PCs\" and \"mobile\" to \"cloud\", with the driving constraint labelled under each era."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}],"$L28","$L29","$L2a","$L2b","$L2c"]}]
26:["$","$L2d",null,{"prev":{"slug":"what-an-os-is","title":"What an Operating System Actually Is","kind":"$undefined","number":1},"next":{"slug":"hardware-an-os-drives","title":"The Hardware an OS Drives","kind":"$undefined","number":3}}]
28:["$","section","02-batch-era",{"id":"02-batch-era","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The batch era & the birth of the OS"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The earliest computers ran exactly one job at a time, and there was no operating system at all. A programmer would sign up for a block of machine time, load their program by hand, often as a deck of punched cards, run it, and read the results. Between jobs the multi-million-dollar machine sat idle while a human shuffled paper, which was an enormous waste."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The first step toward an OS was the batch monitor: a small permanent program that stayed in memory and automatically loaded the next job when the previous one finished. Operators would collect many decks into a batch and hand them off; the monitor sequenced them without a human in the loop between jobs. This was the seed of the OS, software whose job is to run other software."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Batch job"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A program plus its input, submitted as a unit to run without any interaction while it executes."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Monitor"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The first resident control program; it loaded, ran, and cleaned up after each job in sequence."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Job Control Language (JCL)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Early control cards that told the monitor how to run a job, a distant ancestor of the shell script."}]]}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The pain that drove everything next",": "]}],"A batch job that pauses to read a card or write to tape leaves the CPU doing nothing during that I/O. On the most expensive machine in the building, that idle time was intolerable, and fixing it is the whole story of the next section."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L27",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch02%2F02-batch-era.jpg?v=7d61f6cb088c41a404214b942e85c623","alt":"Hand-drawn diagram of an operator feeding a stack of punched-card decks into a mainframe, with a \"monitor\" program in memory sequencing one job after another."}],"$L2e"]}]]}]]}]
29:["$","section","03-multiprogramming-timesharing",{"id":"03-multiprogramming-timesharing","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Multiprogramming & time-sharing"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The fix for the idle CPU was multiprogramming: keep several jobs in memory at the same time, so that when the running job stops to wait on slow I/O, the OS immediately switches the CPU to another job that is ready. The expensive processor now stays busy, and total throughput climbs dramatically. But this demands real OS machinery, memory protection so jobs cannot corrupt each other, and a scheduler to decide who runs next."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Time-sharing was the next leap, and arguably the one that defines the modern OS. Instead of running batch jobs to completion, the OS gives each of many interactive users a rapid slice of CPU time, cycling through them so fast that everyone feels they have the machine to themselves. CTSS pioneered it, the ambitious MULTICS project pushed the ideas further, and UNIX distilled them into something small and elegant that the whole industry ended up building on."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Multiprogramming"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Keeping multiple jobs in memory so the CPU can switch to a ready one whenever the current job blocks on I/O."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Time-sharing"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Rapidly rotating the CPU among many interactive users so each gets a fair, responsive slice."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Time slice (quantum)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The short interval a program is allowed to run before the OS forcibly switches to the next one."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"CTSS / MULTICS / UNIX"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The lineage of time-sharing systems; UNIX became the simple, portable design the modern world inherited."}]]}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The modern echo",": "]}],"Time-sharing is why your laptop runs a browser, an editor, and a music app at once without any of them knowing about the others. The cloud takes the same idea one level up: many isolated tenants sharing one physical server."]}]]}]]}],"$L2f"]}]]}]
2a:["$","section","04-pc-era",{"id":"04-pc-era","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The personal-computer era"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"When the microprocessor made a whole CPU cheap, the computer moved from the shared machine room onto a single person’s desk. The economics inverted overnight: the hardware was no longer the scarce resource, the user’s time and comfort was. Early PC operating systems like DOS were tiny and simple, they ran one program at a time and offered almost no protection, because a machine with one user and one program did not obviously need it."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"As PCs grew more capable, their operating systems had to re-learn everything mainframes already knew. Windows and the Mac line added multitasking, then memory protection, then proper user accounts. Meanwhile UNIX’s ideas flowed into the open-source world: Linux, written from that lineage, became the free UNIX-like kernel that now runs most servers on earth. It is a striking pattern, the PC era rediscovered protection and multitasking from scratch, arriving at the same answers the time-sharing pioneers had found decades earlier."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"DOS"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"An early single-user, single-tasking PC OS with no memory protection, small because the problem was small."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Windows / macOS"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The mainstream desktop lines that gradually added multitasking, protection, and multi-user support."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Linux"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"An open-source, UNIX-like kernel started in 1991; now the backbone of servers, cloud, and Android."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Open source"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Software whose source code is freely shared and modifiable, the model that let Linux spread everywhere."}]]}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Remember",": "]}],"Cheaper hardware did not make OS problems disappear; it just delayed them. The moment PCs ran more than one program and touched a network, they needed the very protections the mainframes had pioneered."]}]]}]]}],"$L30"]}]]}]
2b:["$","section","05-mobile-embedded-realtime",{"id":"05-mobile-embedded-realtime","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Mobile, embedded & real-time"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Phones brought new constraints, battery life, a touch screen, and constant sleeping and waking, but not new fundamentals. Android is built on the Linux kernel, and iOS descends from the same BSD-UNIX family as macOS. They aggressively manage power and suspend background apps, yet underneath they are still scheduling processes, protecting memory, and managing devices exactly like their desktop ancestors."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Embedded and real-time systems flip the priorities entirely. In a pacemaker, an engine controller, or an industrial robot, the machine does not need to run many programs, it needs to react within a guaranteed time. A real-time OS (RTOS) is judged not on throughput but on predictability: can it always respond before the deadline? The distinction between hard and soft real-time is exactly how strict that deadline is."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Android"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A mobile OS built on the Linux kernel, tuned for touch, apps, and battery."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"iOS"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Apple’s mobile OS, descended from the same BSD/UNIX lineage as macOS."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Embedded OS"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A small OS baked into a device (router, TV, sensor) to do one dedicated job reliably."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Hard real-time"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A missed deadline is a system failure, an airbag or brake controller cannot be \"a little late\"."}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Soft real-time"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Deadlines matter but an occasional miss only degrades quality, a video stream may drop a frame."}]]}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":["$L31","$L32"]}]]}],"$L33"]}]]}]
2c:["$","section","06-os-zoo-today",{"id":"06-os-zoo-today","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The OS zoo today"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Put it all together and today’s operating systems fall into a handful of families, each shaped by the constraint it lives under. Desktop and server OSes chase general-purpose performance; mobile OSes chase battery and responsiveness; embedded and real-time systems chase predictability; and distributed and cloud systems chase safe, dense sharing of pooled hardware across many tenants."}],["$","dl","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Desktop / server"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"General-purpose systems (Windows, macOS, Linux) balancing many programs and users."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Mobile"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Battery- and touch-optimised systems (Android, iOS) built on desktop-class kernels."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Embedded / RTOS"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Dedicated, often deadline-driven systems inside appliances, vehicles, and sensors."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Distributed / cloud"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Many machines presented as one pool, sliced into virtual machines and containers for isolated tenants."}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Hypervisor"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Software that runs many virtual machines on one physical host, time-sharing applied to whole operating systems."}]]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"That last family is where the story comes full circle. A hypervisor slices one server into virtual machines, and containers slice one OS kernel into isolated, metered environments, the very same instinct as 1960s time-sharing, now applied to entire operating systems and services. We will unpack VMs and containers properly in a later part; for now, just notice the family resemblance."}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],"$L34"]}]]}],"$L35"]}]]}]
